==============================================================================
PART A: geometry + slot-gradient identities (independent re-run + slot 2)
==============================================================================
[A1] X.X-1/H^2, u.u+1, a.a-a^2, e.e-1, e.u, e.X = 0 , 0 , 0 , 0 , 0 , 0
[A2] Z - (H^2 cosh(ks)+a^2)/k^2 = 0
[A2] (1-Z) + (2H^2/k^2) sinh^2(ks/2) = 0
[A3] W_pullback - DeserLevin(-k^2/16pi^2/sinh^2(ks/2)) = 0   [thermal at T_eff = kappa/2pi]
[A4] e(tau).X(tau-s)   - (a/k^2)(cosh ks - 1) = 0
[A4] e(tau-s).X(tau)   - (a/k^2)(cosh ks - 1) = 0
     => BOTH slot contractions equal the SAME even function: the
        gradient identity holds at EITHER vertex, for EITHER ordering.
[A4] *** IDENTITY (slot 1) ***  e.gradW + a W = 0
[A4] *** IDENTITY (slot 2) ***  e'.gradW + a W = 0
     => with W(s) = <phi(z(tau)) phi(z(tau-s))>:
        <phi' d_e phi> = -a W(-s),  <d_e phi  phi'> = -a W(s):
        each longitudinal-gradient vertex carries EXACTLY one factor a;
        everything else is a function of kappa only.

==============================================================================
PART B: THE NEW LEMMA — the pulled-back field COMMUTATOR is (i/2pi) d'(s),
        EXACTLY, INDEPENDENT of (a,H): dissipation is trajectory-blind
==============================================================================
[B1] Laurent of csch^2(x) about 0: x**6/675 - 2*x**4/189 + x**2/15 - 1/3 + x**(-2)
     W_kappa(s -+ i eps) = -(1/4pi^2)(s -+ i eps)^{-2}  +  [EVEN ANALYTIC
     series in s with kappa-dependent coefficients].  The analytic part is
     CONTINUOUS at s=0, so it cancels between the two i*eps prescriptions:
       C(s) = W(s-i eps) - W(s+i eps)
            = -(1/4pi^2)[(s-i eps)^{-2} - (s+i eps)^{-2}]  ->  (i/2pi) d'(s).
     ALL kappa-dependence cancels: the commutator kernel — hence the exact
     dissipation kernel and the resummed gamma = lambda^2/8pi — is the SAME
     for every (a,H).  [FT check: FT[(i/2pi) d'] = w/2pi = S(w)-S(-w). OK]
[B2] numeric: INT C_eps(s) phi(s) ds  vs  -(i/2pi) phi'(0)  [phi = exp(-s^2)cos(0.7s+0.3)]
